.SH SOURCE FORM
Each rule states its purpose, so it can be judged by whether it achieves it.
.SS Lexical rules
.IP \(bu 3
Bytes 0x20 to 0x7E and newline only.
A byte is then a column, so awk and C measure width with a plain length.
.IP \(bu
No tab characters.
Layout is stated by requests, so every viewer shows the same thing, and tab is free to separate fields in the compiled form.
.IP \(bu
Line 1 is \fB'\e" t\fR when, and only when, the page has a table, so that man runs tbl first.
It is the only line that begins with an apostrophe.
.IP \(bu
A control line is a period followed directly by a request listed below.
A comment is a whole line beginning with a period, a backslash, and a double quote.
.IP \(bu
In fill mode a text line begins with a word: never a space, never empty, and never a period or apostrophe unless written after \e&.
roff treats a leading space or a blank line as a layout instruction; stating breaks with requests gives them one meaning everywhere.
.IP \(bu
One sentence per line is recommended.
Diffs stay small and sentence spacing stays predictable.
.SS Requests
Widths are a bare number or a number followed by n, counting character cells.
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb
l  l  l.
REQUEST|ARGUMENTS|MEANING
_
\&.TH|NAME SEC DATE SOURCE MANUAL|T{
page title; first request; @DATE@ and @VERSION@ are filled in at install and compile
T}
\&.SH .SS|heading|section, subsection
\&.PP .P .LP|none|T{
new paragraph; resets the prevailing indent
T}
\&.TP|[width]|T{
tagged paragraph; the next line, text or a font request, is the tag
T}
\&.IP|[tag [width]]|T{
indented paragraph, optionally tagged
T}
\&.RS|[width]|T{
move the margin in by width or by the prevailing indent
T}
\&.RE|none|restore the margin
\&.nf .fi|none|T{
literal and fill mode; literal blocks hold text only
T}
\&.B .I|words|bold or italic words
T{
\&.BR .RB .IR .RI .BI .IB
T}|words|T{
two fonts alternating word by word
T}
\&.sp|[N]|N blank lines, default 1
\&.br|none|line break
\&.TS .TE|none|table
.TE
.PP
Double quotes group words into one argument; a quote inside is written \e(dq.
A request takes at most 9 arguments.
.SS Escapes
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb
l  l  l.
ESCAPE|MEANING|TERMINAL OUTPUT
_
\efB \efI \efR|bold, italic, roman|font change
\efP|previous font|font change
\e\-|minus, copies as a real hyphen|\-
\ee|backslash|\e
\e&|zero-width guard|nothing
\e(space)|space that never breaks|space
\e(em|em dash|\-\-
\e(en|en dash|\-
\e(bu|bullet|o
\e(aq|apostrophe|\(aq
\e(dq|double quote|"
.TE
.PP
The font returns to roman by the end of every request line and table cell, so emphasis never runs past where it was meant to stop.
.SS Tables
.nf
\&.TS
box tab(@);
lb lb
l  l.
OPTION@MEANING
_
\efB\e\-r\efR@reverse the order
\&.TE
.fi
.PP
The options line is required and ends with a semicolon.
It names the cell separator with tab(c), a printable character that is not a letter or digit and appears in no cell, and may add box, allbox, and center.
Format lines follow, the last ending with a period; keys are l, r, and c, with modifiers b, i, and w(N), a minimum width.
Format line i governs data row i and the last repeats.
Each data row has one cell per column; a row of a single underscore is a rule.
A cell opened with T{ continues on the following lines until a line beginning T}, and is filled within its column.
roff wraps only these text blocks, so any cell that may need wrapping is written as one; the roffsub renderers wrap every cell.
The page installed for man is written by roffsub \-n, which rewrites each table so that roff draws it the same way: an empty separator column between the columns, rules joined by a + in those columns, no vertical lines, and columns holding text blocks widened to fill the line.
.SS Content
A conventional order is NAME as \fIname\fR \e\- \fIsummary\fR, then SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS, EXAMPLES, ENVIRONMENT, FILES, EXIT STATUS, SEE ALSO.
The order is guidance; content belongs to the author.
.SH RENDERING
.SS Width and margins
Text fills to the width with a right margin of 0, or to the measure when one is set.
Words are packed greedily with a ragged right edge; a word too long for its line runs past the margin whole.
Literal lines are never rewrapped.
The indent unit is 7 cells, or 4 when the width is under 60.
.SS Indentation and spacing
.IP \(bu 3
A section heading sits at the margin and its body one unit in; a subsection heading sits half a unit in.
.IP \(bu
Each paragraph, tagged paragraph, and indented paragraph is preceded by one blank line, except directly after a heading.
.IP \(bu
A tag shorter than the prevailing indent shares its line with the body; a tag as long or longer puts the body on the next line.
.IP \(bu
A width given to .TP or .IP becomes the prevailing indent for later items until the next .SH, .SS, or .PP.
.IP \(bu
\&.RS saves the margin and prevailing indent, moves the margin in, and resets the prevailing indent; .RE restores both.
.SS Tables
Tables draw no vertical lines.
Each cell has one space of padding on each side and columns meet at one separator position, a space in data rows and a plus sign in rules, so column text sits three cells apart.
Rules appear where the source has an underscore row; box adds one above and below, allbox one after every row.
.PP
When a table is wider than the space available, the widest columns give up width first, down to a common cap, never below their longest word, and short columns keep their natural width.
Cells wrap within their columns.
When even the longest words do not fit, each row is shown as lines of the form \fIheader\fR: \fIvalue\fR.
.SS Emphasis
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb lb
l  l  l  l.
STYLE|USED FOR|BOLD|ITALIC
_
sgr|a terminal|ESC[1m|ESC[4m, underline
overstrike|a pager|c BS c|_ BS c
plain|a file, a pipe, TERM=dumb|none|none
pre|pre-formatting|\efB|\efI
.TE
.PP
Control bytes are added only as a line is written, so they never count toward its width.
Emphasis is closed before every newline and reopened on the next line.
The SGR parameters may be replaced, so that bold and italic show as colors.
.SH PROGRAM INTERFACE
.SS Help
\fB\-\-help\fR presents the full manual.
What it writes is fixed by four inputs: the embedded manual, the width, the style, and the destination.
The C and bash forms of a program write the same bytes for the same inputs.
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb lb
l  l  l  l.
OUTPUT|GOES TO|STYLE|WIDTH
_
terminal|the pager|overstrike|terminal
terminal, PAGER is cat|the terminal|sgr|terminal
file or pipe|the file or pipe|plain|80
.TE
.PP
The pager is PAGER when set, otherwise less, otherwise more.
The width is MANWIDTH when set, otherwise COLUMNS, otherwise the terminal's own.
ROFFSUB_STYLE, ROFFSUB_SGR, and ROFFSUB_MEASURE override the style, the emphasis parameters, and the measure; TERM=dumb selects plain.
ROFFSUB_HEADING moves section headings that many columns in from the margin, leaving everything else in place.
.SS Usage
\fB\-h\fR serves a different purpose from the manual: a quick reference, a refresher and confirmation when a full review is not needed.
It is composed for that purpose, not extracted from the manual.
.SS Profiles
A man page is built from the source and installed in every case.
Inside the program the manual is carried in one of three profiles.
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb
l  l  l.
PROFILE|THE PROGRAM CARRIES|HELP GIVES
_
full|records and the renderer|T{
any width, emphasis by terminal
T}
preformatted|T{
text set at 80 columns, fonts kept, and the emitter
T}|T{
fixed width, emphasis by terminal
T}
plain|text set at 80 columns|fixed width
.TE
.PP
The full profile is used when the renderer is no larger than the program it would live in, not counting the manual, and an exported bash function stays within its size budget; otherwise preformatted.
Plain is used only when a program asks for it.
.SH COMPILED FORM
One record per line: an operation, then fields separated by tabs.
The operations are th, sh, ss, pp, tp, ip, rs, re, nf, fi, tx, sp, br, and for tables ts, tf, tr, t_, and te; the first record is v 1.
Text fields carry only \efB, \efI, \efR, \e\e, and \e~, and end in roman.
Records are passed to awk on its standard input, never with \-v, which would rewrite their backslashes.
